al fluorescence  detection or or to flex chroma genic  detection or up to 12 flex fluorescence  detection with a serial rejection method  that I won't want to talk about at all  today  and I also won't talk about bay scope  which is a technology that allows you to  visualize very small differences in the  mRNA sequence like at junctions or  splice variants and such but what I will  tell you about is our experience using  RNA scope with the manual fluorescence  assay and so I'll first show you some of  our results with the most important part  which is our sets of controls so each of  the RNA scope assays comes available  with positive controls which are the  probe sets that recognize what you might  consider housekeeping genes so in this  case RNA polymerase 2 epitope role Pro  lil isomerase B and E BIC wouldn't see  and you'll notice in the upper left I  have some violin plots that show the  expression profiles for these three  genes among our single cells so you see  that they're widely expressed but not  uniformly expressed among among all  cells which is which is not uncommon in  the tested the the Fermata genic lineage  often does those things different and  regular somatic cells  but when we look in cross-section  through a segment of test s you can see  in this case there's a seminal first to  Buell in the middle that it's somewhat  ovoid so a little bit a circular but we  can detect RNA polymerase 2 PPI B and  you couldn't see in a good proportion of  the cells within that seminarist tubulin  if we were to zoom in I'm sorry I don't  have a higher magnification you would  see that these genes are detected at  single molecule of resolution you can  kind of see some of the speckled e  labeling for RNA pol ii but for really  highly abundant gens the the dots are so  abundant that they all merge together so  a great example is the acquittance II  where it looks like a hazy red label so  this gave us say confidence that our  tissue was of high quality and we're  able to at least detect these these  abundant and widely expressed  transcripts so is this simply background  well that was our next undertaking using  our negative controls and for that we  use a probe set that recognizes in  irrelevant dean the coli gap B gene  which is not expressed in in eukaryotic  cells and so in this case the the  labeling is essentially nil there's some  background autofluorescence meant Estus  that we can pick up primarily in the  white and green channels in this case  but is not  consistent with single molecule fish  labeling as we saw in the previous slide  so really the the specificity of this of  this technique appears to be pretty high  at least with these these positive and  negative controls so the proof of  principle though was whether we could  get self specific detection of genes  that we know are expressed in there in  particular types of cells within the  test Asst and so we went back to that  panel of genes that I showed earlier  where we could label for some of them at  the protein level box 9 and DDX 4 but  not ID 4 because we lacked a good  antibody so in this case we've detected  these three genes at the RNA level using  RNA scope so in situ hybridization for  ID 4 which we expected to be expressed  based on our single cell results in  spermatocytes off the basement membrane  and so the probe in white in the annals  all the way to the right show the dots  corresponding to ID 4 and you can see  that those cells are one layer of cells  remove the basement membrane and we do  detect I don't have a picture to show  that here we do detect ID 4 in very rare  cells on the basement membrane which are  cells of interest that the spermatic  o'neill stem cells  and importantly we also see that  localization of DD x4 or our germ cell  marker which is most abundantly  expressed in in the mitotic cell  population but also detectable in  spermatogonia is where it's supposed to  be as well as Sox 9 X 9 is the sertoli  cell marker and we see